\section{The \texttt{login} command function}

As stated, if the subcommand is run, we should update the credentials in the 
keyring.
Or the config file, if there is no keyring.
<<functions>>=
def login_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Guides the user to update credentials"""

  print("Enter the hostname for Canvas, "
    "e.g. 'canvas.kth.se' or 'kth.instructure.com'.")
  hostname = input("Canvas hostname: ")

  print(f"""
<<instructions how to get the token>>
""")

  token = input("Canvas token: ")

  try:
    <<save [[hostname]] and [[token]] to keyring>>
  <<fall back to the config file without a keyring>>

<<instructions how to get the token>>=
Open

  https://{hostname}/profile/settings

in your browser. Scroll down to approved integrations and click the
'+ New access token' button. Fill in the required data and click the
'Generate token' button. Enter the token here.
@

Now, to keep this data in the keyring, we simply use [[canvaslms]] as the 
service, then we store the hostname as the password of user 
\enquote{hostname}.
And the same with the token.
<<save [[hostname]] and [[token]] to keyring>>=
keyring.set_password("canvaslms", "hostname", hostname)
keyring.set_password("canvaslms", "token", token)
@

When we need these again, we simply load them.
<<load [[hostname]] and [[token]] from keyring>>=
hostname = keyring.get_password("canvaslms", "hostname")
token = keyring.get_password("canvaslms", "token")
@


\subsection{Falling back to the config file without a keyring}

Not every machine has a working keyring: a headless server, a minimal
container, or a desktop session without a running secret-service daemon all
make [[keyring.set_password]] fail.
We still want [[canvaslms login]] to be useful there, so we fall back to
storing the credentials in the configuration file---in plaintext, since that
file has no encryption of its own.

Because the fallback stores the token in the clear, it must trigger for
exactly one condition: the keyring cannot keep the secret.
The [[keyring]] package raises all of its own failures as subclasses of
[[keyring.errors.KeyringError]]; the no-backend case in particular raises
[[keyring.errors.NoKeyringError]], which is such a subclass.
Catching the base class therefore covers every way the keyring can decline
to store the value---missing backend, locked collection, refusing
backend---and nothing else.

Contrast this with the bare [[except:]] this code used to have.
A bare handler catches [[BaseException]], which includes the two exceptions
that do not report a failure of the operation at all: [[KeyboardInterrupt]]
and [[SystemExit]].
That distinction is not academic here, because [[keyring.set_password]] can
block: with a locked keyring the secret-service daemon prompts the user for
a password and the call does not return until the prompt is answered.
A user who presses Ctrl-C at that prompt means \enquote{stop, don't store
this}---and the bare handler turned that request into its exact opposite,
swallowing the [[KeyboardInterrupt]] and taking the fallback branch, which
wrote the token in plaintext to the config file.
With the narrow handler the [[KeyboardInterrupt]] propagates, the command
aborts, and nothing is written anywhere.

This is why the module outline above imports [[keyring.errors]] explicitly:
the submodule happens to be bound as a side effect of importing the keyring
backends, but a handler that guards a credential should not depend on that
accident.
<<fall back to the config file without a keyring>>=
except keyring.errors.KeyringError:
  canvaslms.cli.warn(f"You don't have a working keyring. "
                     f"Will write hostname and token to config file "
                     f"{args.config_file}.")

  config["canvas"]["host"] = hostname
  config["canvas"]["access_token"] = token

  canvaslms.cli.update_config_file(config, args.config_file)
@

Let's verify both halves of that distinction.
Both tests drive [[login_command]] through its two [[input]] prompts, so we
first need a way to answer them.
<<test functions>>=
def answer_prompts(monkeypatch, *answers):
  """Answers login_command's input() prompts with the given strings."""
  responses = iter(answers)
  monkeypatch.setattr("builtins.input", lambda *args: next(responses))
@

An interrupted keyring call must abort the command and leave nothing behind:
no config file means no plaintext token.
<<test functions>>=
def test_login_aborts_on_keyboard_interrupt(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
  """Ctrl-C during the keyring call must abort, not write the token."""
  answer_prompts(monkeypatch, "canvas.example.com", "secret-token")

  def interrupt(*args, **kwargs):
    raise KeyboardInterrupt

  monkeypatch.setattr(canvaslms.cli.login.keyring, "set_password", interrupt)

  config_file = tmp_path / "config.json"
  args = argparse.Namespace(config_file=str(config_file))

  with pytest.raises(KeyboardInterrupt):
    canvaslms.cli.login.login_command({"canvas": {}}, None, args)

  assert not config_file.exists()
@

A genuine keyring failure, on the other hand, must still leave the user with
working credentials---in a file only they can read.
<<test functions>>=
def test_login_writes_private_config_without_keyring(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
  """Without a keyring backend, credentials go to a user-only config file."""
  answer_prompts(monkeypatch, "canvas.example.com", "secret-token")

  def no_backend(*args, **kwargs):
    raise keyring.errors.NoKeyringError("no backend available")

  monkeypatch.setattr(canvaslms.cli.login.keyring, "set_password", no_backend)

  config_file = tmp_path / "config.json"
  args = argparse.Namespace(config_file=str(config_file))

  canvaslms.cli.login.login_command({"canvas": {}}, None, args)

  config = json.loads(config_file.read_text())
  assert config["canvas"]["host"] == "canvas.example.com"
  assert config["canvas"]["access_token"] == "secret-token"
  assert config_file.stat().st_mode & 0o777 == 0o600
@


\section{Loading user credentials}

The [[load_credentials]] function will try to get the user's LADOK credentials.
There are three locations:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the system keyring,
\item the environment variables [[CANVAS_SERVER]] and [[CANVAS_TOKEN]],
\item the configuration file.
\end{enumerate}
They are given the priority they are listed in above.
It tries to fetch the credentials in the given order; if it succeeds, it 
returns those credentials, otherwise it tries the next.
If all fail, the function will return [[None]] for both.
(This is due to how we handle the [[login]] command.)
<<functions>>=
def load_credentials(config):
  """Load credentials from keyring, environment or config dictionary"""
  try:
    <<load [[hostname]] and [[token]] from keyring>>
    if hostname and token:
      return hostname, token
  except:
    pass

  try:
    hostname = os.environ["CANVAS_SERVER"]
    token = os.environ["CANVAS_TOKEN"]
    return hostname, token
  except KeyError:
    pass

  try:
    return config["canvas"]["host"], config["canvas"]["access_token"]
  except KeyError:
    pass

  return None, None
@

